<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asirgarh Fort</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Asirgarh is a hill fortress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burhanpur">Burhanpur</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, commanding the pass between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> and Tapti valleys and long called the key to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. The Faruqi rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khandesh">Khandesh</a> held it from the early 15th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s Mughal army besieged it, and it surrendered in January 1601. It later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and in 1819 to the British.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asirgarh-fort.jpg" alt="Asirgarh Fort" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yashasvi nagda · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asirgarh_fort_-Asirgarh-Burhanpur-(Madhya_Pradesh,_India).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
